  "enhanced_information": "In the case of a patient with a deep second degree flame burn covering 50% of body surface area, the following measures are evaluated: 1) Absolute diet is contraindicated due to the risk of ileus and gastrointestinal complications in acute burn care. 2) Administration of lactated Ringer's solution according to the Parkland formula is indicated for fluid resuscitation to prevent hypovolemic shock. 3) Prophylactic administration of intravenous antibiotics is not standard practice unless there is evidence of infection, as routine use can lead to resistance and other complications. 4) Heparin administration for antithrombotic prophylaxis may be considered to prevent thromboembolism in patients with extensive burns. Therefore, the measure that would NOT be indicated is the absolute diet.",
  "analysis": "In the emergency treatment of a patient with a deep second degree flame burn covering 50% of body surface area, several measures are evaluated. The absolute diet (option opa) is contraindicated due to the risk of ileus and gastrointestinal complications, making it the least appropriate choice. The administration of lactated Ringer's solution according to the Parkland formula (option opb) is a standard practice for fluid resuscitation in burn patients to prevent hypovolemic shock. Prophylactic administration of intravenous antibiotics (option opc) is not routinely indicated unless there is evidence of infection, as it can lead to antibiotic resistance. Heparin administration for antithrombotic prophylaxis (option opd) may be considered in extensive burns to prevent thromboembolism. Therefore, the measure that would NOT be indicated is the absolute diet, as it poses more risks than benefits in this acute setting.",
